
  Throughout the 17th and 18th centuries, the slide rule underwent gradual refinement. Various mathematicians and
  instrument makers improved the design, adding additional scales and developing specialized slide rules for specific
  applications. The basic principle remained constant: aligned logarithmic scales allowed users to add lengths on the
  rule corresponding to multiplying or dividing the actual numbers, while other scales handled squares, square roots,
  trigonometric functions, and logarithms themselves. The elegance of this mechanical solution to mathematical problems
  captivated the scientific community.

  The 19th century witnessed the slide rule's transition from specialist's tool to universal instrument. As
  industrialization accelerated, engineers and surveyors increasingly relied on slide rules for practical calculations
  in the field. Improvements in manufacturing made slide rules more affordable and accessible to students and
  professionals. The addition of the cursor—a movable hair

